WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.619 --> 00:00:16.360
Ambition: it can spur a man to greatness, but it can also turn a good man to evil.

2
00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:22.762
The story of Macbeth, who became King of Scotland through murder, is the story of one such man.

3
00:00:23.402 --> 00:00:28.224
The story begins on a barren Scottish heath where three witches are making their plans.

4
00:00:29.412 --> 00:00:31.553
"Where shall we three meet again?

5
00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:34.414
In thunder, lightning, or in rain?"

6
00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:36.295
asked the first witch.

7
00:00:36.315 --> 00:00:44.578
"Before the setting of the sun, when the battle is lost and won, upon the heath to meet

8
00:00:44.798 --> 00:00:47.660
Macbeth!" the second witch answered.

9
00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:56.403
The third one chanted, "Fair is foul, and foul is fair," and they disappeared into the filthy fog.

10
00:00:57.364 --> 00:01:02.931
At the same time, at a camp near a battlefield, three men were also talking about Macbeth.

11
00:01:03.831 --> 00:01:08.895
King Duncan of Scotland and Ross, one of his noblemen, were listening to a wounded soldier.

12
00:01:08.915 --> 00:01:13.097
"At first, the battle's outcome was uncertain," the soldier said.

13
00:01:13.117 --> 00:01:21.342
"The rebels swarmed around us, but our two captains, Macbeth and Banquo, beat them back madly.

14
00:01:21.863 --> 00:01:23.604
They were like two lions among kittens!

15
00:01:24.016 --> 00:01:29.057
Then, with his sword, brave Macbeth carved a bloody path through the enemy until he faced the thane

16
00:01:29.077 --> 00:01:29.698
of Cawdor.

17
00:01:30.738 --> 00:01:34.839
With two strokes, he cut down the traitorous leader and returned carrying his head."

18
00:01:34.859 --> 00:01:40.481
"The victory is ours," said Ross, after the soldier was carried out.

19
00:01:40.501 --> 00:01:43.221
"Your kinsman, Macbeth, fought bravely."

20
00:01:43.241 --> 00:01:49.063
"Yes," said Duncan, "and what the traitor has lost, Macbeth has gained!

21
00:01:49.583 --> 00:01:53.952
I have decided that Macbeth will be the new thane of Cawdor."

22
00:01:53.972 --> 00:01:58.834
As Banquo and Macbeth rode back from the battle, the sunlight was swallowed up by a sudden fog.

23
00:01:58.854 --> 00:02:03.396
"I have never seen such a foul and fair day," Macbeth said.

24
00:02:03.996 --> 00:02:04.556
They stopped.

25
00:02:05.197 --> 00:02:09.018
Before them, pointing their crooked fingers at Macbeth, stood the three witches.

26
00:02:09.819 --> 00:02:11.199
"What are these withered creatures?"

27
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:12.600
said Banquo.

28
00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.021
"Speak, if you can," called Macbeth.

29
00:02:16.942 --> 00:02:17.522
"What are you?"

30
00:02:17.542 --> 00:02:20.783
"All hail Macbeth!"

31
00:02:21.684 --> 00:02:22.484
said the first witch.

32
00:02:23.636 --> 00:02:28.740
"Hail to thee, Macbeth, Thane of Cawdor," said the second.

33
00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:35.865
With a voice like a crow, the third witch croaked, "Hail Macbeth, who shall be king!"

34
00:02:37.166 --> 00:02:44.411
As Macbeth stood in bewilderment, the witches turned to Banquo and said, "Lesser than Macbeth, and greater.

35
00:02:45.011 --> 00:02:48.394
Not you, but your descendants shall be kings."

36
00:02:49.875 --> 00:02:51.616
Then they vanished, melting into the air.

37
00:02:52.372 --> 00:02:53.612
The two men were astounded.

38
00:02:54.273 --> 00:02:55.313
What did these words mean?

39
00:02:56.053 --> 00:02:58.494
Had it all been just a trick of their tired minds?

40
00:02:59.334 --> 00:03:02.355
The sun broke through the fog, and Ross rode up to meet them.

41
00:03:02.375 --> 00:03:08.957
"Macbeth, the king has heard the news of your success and thanks you," Ross said.

42
00:03:08.977 --> 00:03:14.078
"He has asked me to name you most worthy Thane of Cawdor.

43
00:03:14.538 --> 00:03:16.459
That title is now yours."

44
00:03:17.519 --> 00:03:19.700
A perplexed look came over Macbeth's face.

45
00:03:20.224 --> 00:03:22.205
The second witch's words had just come true!

46
00:03:22.985 --> 00:03:25.886
Did this mean the third witch's prophecy would also come true?

47
00:03:26.406 --> 00:03:27.206
He would be king!

48
00:03:27.226 --> 00:03:31.047
"Banquo," he whispered, "did you hear?

49
00:03:31.747 --> 00:03:33.028
I am the thane of Cawdor!

50
00:03:33.868 --> 00:03:36.289
Don't you hope that your children will be kings?"

51
00:03:36.309 --> 00:03:39.870
"It may not be," Banquo whispered back.

52
00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:44.291
"Sometimes the devil's servants tempt us with truth to lead us into evil."

53
00:03:45.471 --> 00:03:46.411
Ross spoke up again.

54
00:03:46.932 --> 00:03:51.110
He said that King Duncan would dine at Macbeth's castle to honor him in person.

55
00:03:51.891 --> 00:03:54.793
As Macbeth headed home, thoughts rushed through his mind.

56
00:03:54.813 --> 00:03:56.675
"I shall be king!"

57
00:03:57.015 --> 00:03:58.036
he repeated to himself.

58
00:03:58.716 --> 00:04:00.858
But how could this happen while King Duncan lived?

59
00:04:01.399 --> 00:04:02.159
It would take murder.

60
00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.441
No, that was too horrible!

61
00:04:05.002 --> 00:04:08.224
Luck had made him the thane of Cawdor; luck would make him king.

62
00:04:09.145 --> 00:04:12.628
Macbeth arrived at his castle and told his wife everything that had happened.

63
00:04:13.368 --> 00:04:14.569
Lady Macbeth was elated.

64
00:04:14.589 --> 00:04:21.871
"My dearest partner in greatness," she told him, "you shall have what the witches promised."

65
00:04:23.232 --> 00:04:26.515
Her husband went off to remove his armor and clean off the grime of the battle.

66
00:04:27.316 --> 00:04:29.578
Lady Macbeth stood alone in the great hall, thinking.

67
00:04:29.598 --> 00:04:36.444
"My husband has ambition," she said to herself, "but not the villainy to go with it.

68
00:04:37.204 --> 00:04:41.488
He is too full of human kindness to take the quickest path to greatness.

69
00:04:42.469 --> 00:04:46.172
With my tongue, I'll pour unholy courage into his ear!"

70
00:04:47.264 --> 00:04:50.486
Just then a trumpet sounded to signal that King Duncan had arrived.

71
00:04:50.966 --> 00:04:53.768
He was accompanied by his two sons and many noblemen.

72
00:04:54.988 --> 00:04:57.550
Lady Macbeth heard the servants running to open the gate.

73
00:04:58.530 --> 00:04:59.311
Night was falling.

74
00:04:59.751 --> 00:05:03.933
She went to a window and looked up at the dark clouds, her mind on murder.

75
00:05:03.953 --> 00:05:07.015
"Come, dark night," she called.

76
00:05:07.035 --> 00:05:10.357
"Make my blood run thick and cold!"

77
00:05:11.357 --> 00:05:14.279
Macbeth entered the hall and Lady Macbeth turned from the window.

78
00:05:14.299 --> 00:05:17.288
"The king has arrived," she said.

79
00:05:17.308 --> 00:05:21.770
"Good," said Macbeth, "He will sleep here tonight."

80
00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:24.991
"Ah, the future is ours!"

81
00:05:25.712 --> 00:05:26.132
she said.

82
00:05:26.792 --> 00:05:31.934
As they walked to the courtyard, Lady Macbeth explained to her husband how easy it would be to

83
00:05:31.974 --> 00:05:33.435
murder Duncan while he slept.

84
00:05:34.235 --> 00:05:37.536
"He shall not see tomorrow's sun," she said.

85
00:05:38.477 --> 00:05:42.658
Macbeth thought again of the witches' promise, looked at his wife, and agreed.

86
00:05:43.639 --> 00:05:46.300
King Duncan smiled broadly as they approached.

87
00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:50.181
"Your castle has a sweet and pleasant air," he said.

88
00:05:50.761 --> 00:05:53.862
He hugged Macbeth and turned to face the gathered noblemen.

89
00:05:54.682 --> 00:05:59.743
Banquo was there and Ross, as well as the king's sons, Malcolm and Donaldbain.

90
00:06:00.364 --> 00:06:04.665
In a loud voice for all to hear, Duncan thanked Macbeth for his bravery and service.

91
00:06:05.385 --> 00:06:09.706
Then the king took Lady Macbeth's arm and chatted pleasantly with her as they walked ahead.

92
00:06:10.546 --> 00:06:11.787
Macbeth walked by himself.

93
00:06:11.807 --> 00:06:16.368
"If it were done, it should be done quickly," he said to himself.

94
00:06:17.252 --> 00:06:21.694
"Oh, my thoughts are evil but with one blow─success!

95
00:06:22.314 --> 00:06:24.695
And success would end these troubled thoughts."

96
00:06:26.056 --> 00:06:31.038
In the hall, Macbeth saw his wife and the king sitting together and smiling, and his thoughts changed.

97
00:06:31.058 --> 00:06:34.379
"I am Duncan's kinsman and his host.

98
00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:39.182
I should be protecting him from murder, not planning to use the knife myself!"

99
00:06:40.142 --> 00:06:44.564
After the feast, Macbeth told his wife, "This business will go no further."

100
00:06:45.664 --> 00:06:47.264
"Has your courage fallen asleep?"

101
00:06:47.825 --> 00:06:48.245
she asked.

102
00:06:48.965 --> 00:06:49.585
"Quiet, woman!

103
00:06:50.285 --> 00:06:52.766
I dare to do anything that makes a man a man!"

104
00:06:52.786 --> 00:06:56.387
"Then be more than a man, be king!"

105
00:06:56.747 --> 00:06:57.587
said Lady Macbeth.

106
00:06:59.208 --> 00:07:04.229
She explained how, during dinner, she had made sure that Duncan's two servants drank too much wine.

107
00:07:04.989 --> 00:07:07.630
They would sleep heavily, leaving Duncan unguarded.

108
00:07:08.650 --> 00:07:12.471
It would be easy to kill Duncan and then cover the servants with his blood in order to

109
00:07:12.531 --> 00:07:13.511
frame them for the murder.

110
00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:16.280
"My wife!"

111
00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:17.521
Macbeth said with admiration.

112
00:07:17.541 --> 00:07:20.204
"I swear, there's nothing soft in you!

113
00:07:20.624 --> 00:07:21.224
It is settled.

114
00:07:21.785 --> 00:07:23.586
Let's attempt this terrible task."

115
00:07:24.607 --> 00:07:29.612
When all the candles were out and the castle was dark, Macbeth crept quietly toward the room where

116
00:07:29.632 --> 00:07:30.332
Duncan slept.

117
00:07:31.293 --> 00:07:32.514
He turned a corner and stopped.

118
00:07:33.074 --> 00:07:35.096
Something seemed to float in the hallway ahead of him.

119
00:07:35.116 --> 00:07:37.919
"Is this a dagger which I see before me?"

120
00:07:38.659 --> 00:07:39.039
he said.

121
00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:42.562
But when he reached out to grab it, his hand passed right through it.

122
00:07:43.723 --> 00:07:46.710
Slowly, the faintly glowing dagger led him forward.

123
00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:50.792
"It is nothing but a dagger of the mind," he told himself.

124
00:07:51.653 --> 00:07:52.133
"But look!

125
00:07:52.654 --> 00:07:55.916
It has the exact form of the dagger I've pulled from my belt.

126
00:07:56.817 --> 00:07:59.619
And now blood is dripping from its ghostly blade."

127
00:08:00.519 --> 00:08:01.220
He shook his head.

128
00:08:01.240 --> 00:08:04.762
"It is nothing─just a figment of my imagination."

129
00:08:05.583 --> 00:08:06.203
He looked down.

130
00:08:06.784 --> 00:08:12.168
The dagger had led him past Duncan's sleeping servants, and there before him was the king, lying peacefully

131
00:08:12.208 --> 00:08:12.508
in bed.

132
00:08:13.556 --> 00:08:15.137
A bell began to sound in the distance.

133
00:08:16.018 --> 00:08:22.782
"Hear it not, Duncan," Macbeth whispered, "for this bell summons you either to heaven or to hell."

134
00:08:23.942 --> 00:08:28.145
And before the bell could wake the sleeping king, Macbeth plunged his dagger down.
